WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.719 --> 00:00:05.459
Marvel's WandaVision has finally wrapped up
the first big chapter of the MCU's phase four.

00:00:05.459 --> 00:00:10.010
The grand finale left us with lots of answers,
several new questions, a killer costume change,

00:00:10.010 --> 00:00:11.700
and a few great fights.

00:00:11.700 --> 00:00:14.800
Let's break down the small details you may
have missed in all the madness.

00:00:16.020 --> 00:00:19.640
WandaVision hasn't exactly been an action
spectacle, but its finale starts off with

00:00:19.640 --> 00:00:20.640
a bang.

00:00:20.640 --> 00:00:24.340
On the suburban streets of Westview with Agatha
holding Billy and Tommy hostage, the episode's

00:00:24.340 --> 00:00:26.660
opening sequence sees Agatha and Wanda clash.

00:00:26.660 --> 00:00:30.811
While it won't be until later in the episode
that Wanda shows what she can really do, early

00:00:30.811 --> 00:00:34.640
in her battle with the older witch, Wanda
uses a trick she likely learned from fighting

00:00:34.640 --> 00:00:39.370
Iron Man in Captain America: Civil War — rather
than using her powers on Agatha directly,

00:00:39.370 --> 00:00:43.000
she taps into her telekinesis to slam a car
right into Agatha.

00:00:43.000 --> 00:00:46.430
When Wanda approaches the house to see what's
happened to her opponent, she sees Agatha's

00:00:46.430 --> 00:00:48.570
boots sticking out from beneath the car.

00:00:48.570 --> 00:00:51.260
As she inches closer, she sees the boots are
empty.

00:00:51.260 --> 00:00:56.190
The moment references 1939's The Wizard of
Oz, which includes the famous visual of a

00:00:56.190 --> 00:00:58.620
house landing on top of the Wicked Witch of
the East.

00:00:58.620 --> 00:01:03.390
Of course, unlike the witch in the earlier
classic, Agatha isn't dead or defeated.

00:01:03.390 --> 00:01:07.050
That's not the only reference to a non-MCU
movie in the episode, either.

00:01:07.050 --> 00:01:11.390
As Wanda, Vision, Billy, and Tommy face off
against the military in the town square, it's

00:01:11.390 --> 00:01:15.380
a shot very reminiscent of the moment in The
Incredibles when the family faces off against

00:01:15.380 --> 00:01:16.540
Syndrome's minions.

00:01:17.540 --> 00:01:21.210
There were a lot of rumors out there indicating
that Benedict Cumberbatch might make a cameo

00:01:21.210 --> 00:01:23.450
in the WandaVision finale as Doctor Strange.

00:01:23.450 --> 00:01:28.990
Well, the finale came and went without any
sign from the resident of 177A Bleecker Street,

00:01:28.990 --> 00:01:31.360
but that doesn't mean he was forgotten entirely.

00:01:31.360 --> 00:01:35.409
As the battle between Wanda and Agatha moves
to the Westview town center, Agatha confirms

00:01:35.409 --> 00:01:39.560
what many of us have suspected since the end
of episode 7 — the tome she was keeping

00:01:39.560 --> 00:01:41.270
in her basement is the infamous Darkhold.

00:01:41.270 --> 00:01:42.290
Before revealing the book itself, Agatha tells
Wanda,

00:01:42.290 --> 00:01:43.290
"Did you know there's an entire chapter devoted
to you in the Darkhold?

00:01:43.290 --> 00:01:44.290
That's the book of the Damned."

00:01:44.290 --> 00:01:46.430
Ignoring Wanda's protests, Agatha reads from
the book, saying that the Scarlet Witch is

00:01:46.430 --> 00:01:47.860
"not born, she is forged.

00:01:47.860 --> 00:01:50.850
She has no coven, nor need for incantation."

00:01:50.850 --> 00:01:52.979
Perhaps the most impressive thing Agatha reads
is that:

00:01:52.979 --> 00:01:57.260
"Your power exceeds that of the Sorcerer Supreme."

00:01:57.260 --> 00:02:01.850
As we know, Doctor Strange is the Sorcerer
Supreme, and he's not exactly a street magician.

00:02:01.850 --> 00:02:05.700
The argument could be made that of all the
heroes to go one-on-one with Thanos in Avengers:

00:02:05.700 --> 00:02:09.200
Infinity War, none make as good a showing
as Doctor Strange.

00:02:09.200 --> 00:02:12.670
Of course, Wanda gives as good as she gets
when she shows up to settle her score with

00:02:12.670 --> 00:02:17.780
the villain in Endgame, but still — saying
Wanda is more powerful than Strange is significant.

00:02:18.780 --> 00:02:22.040
Of the many things Wanda does in her battle
against Agatha, she tries something we haven't

00:02:22.040 --> 00:02:23.530
seen in quite a while.

00:02:23.530 --> 00:02:27.520
Appearing behind Agatha, she taps into Agatha's
mind to uproot her nightmares.

00:02:27.520 --> 00:02:29.319
Things don't quite go as planned.

00:02:29.319 --> 00:02:33.470
While Wanda does unearth the vision of Agatha's
old coven trying to kill her, the more experienced

00:02:33.470 --> 00:02:35.610
witch manages to turn the tables on her.

00:02:35.610 --> 00:02:39.280
You can't blame Wanda for trying, since this
is a move that worked out well for her in

00:02:39.280 --> 00:02:42.930
Avengers: Age of Ultron, when we see Wanda
use this power successfully on almost all

00:02:42.930 --> 00:02:43.930
of the Avengers.

00:02:44.930 --> 00:02:49.110
We get to see Wanda in a comic book accurate
get-up in episode 6, but, just as is often

00:02:49.110 --> 00:02:53.050
the case in the MCU, that costume was intentionally
made to look pretty goofy.

00:02:53.050 --> 00:02:56.959
Similarly, the costumes worn by Vision and
the fake Pietro don't come off like what you'd

00:02:56.959 --> 00:02:59.450
expect to see the Avengers wearing while defending
Earth.

00:02:59.450 --> 00:03:03.180
But while we get hints of it in the penultimate
episode, we don't get to see Wanda in her

00:03:03.180 --> 00:03:07.400
new and improved comics-accurate outfit until
the series finale, and it's worth the wait.

00:03:07.400 --> 00:03:12.070
We first see an energy outline of the headpiece
forming while Wanda is in Agatha's nightmare

00:03:12.070 --> 00:03:13.070
vision.

00:03:13.070 --> 00:03:16.730
After Wanda places the runes of power along
her Hex boundary and she accepts who she is,

00:03:16.730 --> 00:03:19.420
we finally get to see her transformation begin.

00:03:19.420 --> 00:03:23.200
Once she reclaims her power from Agatha, she
descends from the sky above Westview in an

00:03:23.200 --> 00:03:26.920
outfit that reflects her comic book attire
— and doesn't look like it was thrown together

00:03:26.920 --> 00:03:28.879
at the last minute for a costume party.

00:03:30.011 --> 00:03:33.990
In his final moments with Wanda, Vision offers
words of comfort and hope, speculating that

00:03:33.990 --> 00:03:38.130
perhaps — in spite of everything — this
won't be the last time they see one another.

00:03:38.130 --> 00:03:39.130
Vision says to Wanda,

00:03:39.130 --> 00:03:43.840
"I have been a voice with no body, a body
but not human, and now a memory made real.

00:03:43.840 --> 00:03:47.690
“Who knows what I might be next?"

00:03:47.690 --> 00:03:52.730
When he calls himself a "voice with no body,"
Vision is referring to his time spent as J.A.R.V.I.S.,

00:03:52.730 --> 00:03:54.230
Tony Stark's A.I. assistant.

00:03:54.230 --> 00:03:58.980
While he doesn't appear in person in the MCU
until Avengers: Age of Ultron, Bettany voiced

00:03:58.980 --> 00:04:03.569
J.A.R.V.I.S. in the first two Iron Man films
and the first two Avengers films.

00:04:03.569 --> 00:04:06.820
"Congratulations, sir.

00:04:06.820 --> 00:04:08.910
You have created a new element."

00:04:08.910 --> 00:04:12.890
Unlike his comic book counterpart, whose brainwaves
are based on those of the superhero Wonder

00:04:12.890 --> 00:04:16.699
Man, Vision's personality is something of
a fusion of different characters, including

00:04:16.699 --> 00:04:17.699
J.A.R.V.I.S.

00:04:17.699 --> 00:04:18.699
That J.A.R.V.I.S.

00:04:18.699 --> 00:04:21.049
helps to create Vision isn't exactly an obscure
fact.

00:04:21.049 --> 00:04:24.470
But considering how much the character has
been through since then, it can be very easy

00:04:24.470 --> 00:04:28.690
to forget that in a sense, Vision has been
with the MCU narrative since the very beginning.

00:04:29.690 --> 00:04:33.210
By the end of WandaVision, we're still not
completely clear on what Monica Rambeau's

00:04:33.210 --> 00:04:36.350
repeated exposure to Wanda's Hex has done
to her.

00:04:36.350 --> 00:04:40.380
After she pierces the Hex boundary a second
time in episode 7, it's clear that she's more

00:04:40.380 --> 00:04:41.800
than she used to be.

00:04:41.800 --> 00:04:44.280
What isn't clear is what that means exactly.

00:04:44.280 --> 00:04:48.220
In episode 7, we see her eyes glow blue a
number of times, and she recovers from an

00:04:48.220 --> 00:04:50.900
assault by Wanda quicker than you would expect.

00:04:50.900 --> 00:04:54.870
But in the series finale, we get a clue that
her abilities could be close to those of her

00:04:54.870 --> 00:04:56.280
comic book counterpart.

00:04:56.280 --> 00:05:01.410
In the comics, Monica Rambeau has the ability
to transform herself into any form of energy.

00:05:01.410 --> 00:05:04.750
Particularly back in the '80s when she first
showed up, Rambeau would often appear covered

00:05:04.750 --> 00:05:08.150
in a golden hue as she transformed into light
itself.

00:05:08.150 --> 00:05:12.139
Along with allowing her to travel impossibly
fast, while she was in this form, solid objects

00:05:12.139 --> 00:05:14.380
would pass through her without harming her.

00:05:14.380 --> 00:05:18.850
We see something like that power in WandaVision
When Hayward tries to shoot Billy and Tommy.

00:05:18.850 --> 00:05:22.520
Monica steps between the director and the
boys, and her eyes glow gold.

00:05:22.520 --> 00:05:24.810
As the bullets reach her, they pass through
her harmlessly.

00:05:24.810 --> 00:05:29.880
At the same time, the bullets are slowed down
— and Monica's entire body takes on a semi-transparent

00:05:29.880 --> 00:05:30.880
golden appearance.

00:05:32.090 --> 00:05:36.110
Back in episode 4, Jimmy Woo lists the Skrulls
as one of the possible culprits behind the

00:05:36.110 --> 00:05:37.450
Westview Hex.

00:05:37.450 --> 00:05:39.850
It turns out, of course, they have nothing
to do with it.

00:05:39.850 --> 00:05:43.530
But that doesn't mean they were going to completely
keep their noses out of what was going on.

00:05:43.530 --> 00:05:46.910
In the series finale's mid-credits scene,
the authorities are wrapping things up in

00:05:46.910 --> 00:05:50.350
Westview when a woman leads Monica into a
theater under false pretenses.

00:05:50.350 --> 00:05:53.960
The woman ultimately reveals herself to be
a Skrull and tells Monica,

00:05:53.960 --> 00:05:56.370
"I was sent by an old friend of your mother's."

00:05:56.370 --> 00:05:59.340
She tells Monica her old friend wants to meet
with her.

00:05:59.340 --> 00:06:02.490
When Monica asks where, the Skrull points
meaningfully upward.

00:06:02.490 --> 00:06:07.220
In most likelihood, the "old friend" the Skrull
is referring to is Talos, the Skrull who works

00:06:07.220 --> 00:06:12.040
with Monica's mother Maria, Carol Danvers,
and Nick Fury in Captain Marvel, and who subsequently

00:06:12.040 --> 00:06:15.240
has a mid-credits cameo in Spider-Man: Far
from Home.

00:06:15.240 --> 00:06:17.040
It could also be Fury himself.

00:06:17.040 --> 00:06:20.800
Whoever this old friend is, we'll likely find
out in the upcoming Captain Marvel 2.

00:06:22.229 --> 00:06:25.800
If you stick around for not just the mid-credits
scene but the post-credits scene as well,

00:06:25.800 --> 00:06:29.699
then you see Wanda in a cabin in some distant
wilderness — looking pretty similar to Bruce

00:06:29.699 --> 00:06:33.310
Banner's hideout at the end of 2008's Incredible
Hulk.

00:06:33.310 --> 00:06:37.070
Guarding the cabin is an illusion of a "normal"
Wanda, while inside the building we see the

00:06:37.070 --> 00:06:41.419
Scarlet Witch's astral form accessing her
powers and poring over the Darkhold, presumably

00:06:41.419 --> 00:06:45.650
to take Agatha's advice and learn more about
who she is and what she's capable of.

00:06:45.650 --> 00:06:49.039
Just before the scene ends, we hear Billy
and Tommy's voices calling out to Wanda for

00:06:49.039 --> 00:06:50.039
help.

00:06:50.039 --> 00:06:53.460
This is pretty significant because in the
comics, while Wanda's twins are revealed to

00:06:53.460 --> 00:06:57.610
be constructs of her power, they are reincarnated
in the forms of the boys who become the heroes

00:06:57.610 --> 00:06:59.210
Wiccan and Speed.

00:06:59.210 --> 00:07:02.810
While it's too soon to tell, it seems likely
this could be exactly what leads Wanda to

00:07:02.810 --> 00:07:07.877
join forces with the Sorcerer Supreme in Doctor
Strange in the Multiverse of Madness.

00:07:07.877 --> 00:07:12.460
WandaVision's eighth episode did a lot to explain the mystery at the heart of the MCU series --

00:07:12.460 --> 00:07:16.221
namely, why did Wanda make herself the center of a strange, surreal sitcom?

00:07:16.221 --> 00:07:21.168
Let's dig even deeper into some of the easter eggs and references in Wanda's real-life TV Land.

00:07:21.168 --> 00:07:25.169
In episode 8 of WandaVision, Wanda is made
to see the night that her life was ripped

00:07:25.169 --> 00:07:29.340
apart in Sokovia . Moments before a fateful
explosion, she and her family are watching

00:07:29.340 --> 00:07:33.710
her favorite episode of The Dick Van Dyke
Show – season 2's "It May Look Like A Walnut."

00:07:33.710 --> 00:07:37.780
This is a classic episode of the series, as
Rob Petrie watches a science fiction film

00:07:37.780 --> 00:07:40.360
about walnut-obsessed aliens infiltrating
Earth.

00:07:40.360 --> 00:07:43.040
The next day, all of the events of the film
seem to come true.

00:07:43.040 --> 00:07:47.289
First, his wife turns into a space invader,
and then Rob himself begins to morph into

00:07:47.289 --> 00:07:48.289
an alien.

00:07:48.289 --> 00:07:52.430
The episode was a parody of the sci-fi classic
Invasion of the Body Snatchers, only with

00:07:52.430 --> 00:07:55.490
movie star Danny Thomas as the extraterrestrial
ringleader.

00:07:55.490 --> 00:07:59.800
The Easter egg at play is that this Dick Van
Dyke episode is really just a bad dream.

00:07:59.800 --> 00:08:02.039
Rob's wife hasn't been taken by the aliens.

00:08:02.039 --> 00:08:05.010
He's not missing his thumbs or lost his sense
of humor.

00:08:05.010 --> 00:08:08.419
Similarly, Wanda takes all of the bad things
that have ever happened to her and turns them

00:08:08.419 --> 00:08:09.639
into a bad dream.

00:08:10.639 --> 00:08:14.620
Thanks to Agatha Harkness, Wanda experiences
a vivid flashback of her childhood in episode

00:08:14.620 --> 00:08:18.980
8, and for the first time, we see the incident
that radicalized Wanda and Pietro and drove

00:08:18.980 --> 00:08:20.750
them toward joining HYDRA.

00:08:20.750 --> 00:08:24.690
In the middle of TV night, a missile rips
through their house, killing their parents.

00:08:24.690 --> 00:08:27.980
Pietro grabs Wanda and pulls her under the
bed, keeping her safe.

00:08:27.980 --> 00:08:31.319
But that's when another missile lands three
feet in front of them ... but it doesn't go

00:08:31.319 --> 00:08:32.319
off.

00:08:32.319 --> 00:08:36.110
The siblings stay trapped under the bed for
two days before they move.

00:08:36.110 --> 00:08:40.179
And as the duo mention in Avengers: Age of
Ultron, the rocket has a very familiar word

00:08:40.179 --> 00:08:41.489
painted on the side:

00:08:41.489 --> 00:08:46.309
"On the side of the shell was painted one
word."

00:08:46.309 --> 00:08:47.629
"Stark."

00:08:47.629 --> 00:08:51.550
That spurs their hatred of Tony Stark, pushing
them into the arms of HYDRA as they volunteer

00:08:51.550 --> 00:08:54.079
for the experiments that would give them their
powers.

00:08:54.079 --> 00:08:57.070
Up until this episode, however, we'd only
heard of this tragic event.

00:08:57.070 --> 00:09:01.290
But the scene in WandaVision goes into greater
detail, including a reveal that the shell

00:09:01.290 --> 00:09:03.389
actually said "Stark Industries."

00:09:03.389 --> 00:09:07.749
More importantly, Agatha questions Wanda as
to whether she ever wondered if it was a coincidence

00:09:07.749 --> 00:09:09.050
that the bomb didn't go off.

00:09:09.050 --> 00:09:11.899
"Did you stop that bomb?"

00:09:11.899 --> 00:09:12.899
"What?"

00:09:12.899 --> 00:09:14.990
"You used a probability hex."

00:09:14.990 --> 00:09:18.670
From her perspective, it's obvious that Wanda
was a "baby witch" who subconsciously used

00:09:18.670 --> 00:09:21.870
a probability-altering spell to prevent the
explosive from going off.

00:09:23.019 --> 00:09:28.160
During one of episode 8's flashback sequences,
we see the Mind Stone float out towards Wanda.

00:09:28.160 --> 00:09:32.120
As a result, she sees an image of a woman
in silhouette, with flowing hair and a headband

00:09:32.120 --> 00:09:35.250
very similar to the classic Scarlet Witch
costume.

00:09:35.250 --> 00:09:37.660
None of this is recorded by HYDRA's cameras.

00:09:37.660 --> 00:09:40.260
What exactly this is referring to is unclear.

00:09:40.260 --> 00:09:44.200
Is the Mind Stone seeing Wanda's future where
she fully embraces her identity as the Scarlet

00:09:44.200 --> 00:09:45.200
Witch?

00:09:45.200 --> 00:09:46.870
Or is it a reference to her heritage?

00:09:46.870 --> 00:09:51.149
In the comics, Wanda learned only relatively
recently that her mother was actually Natalya

00:09:51.149 --> 00:09:54.269
Maximoff, one in a long line of Scarlet Witches.

00:09:54.269 --> 00:09:58.170
Whatever the case may be, this is the point
that Wanda first comes into her powers.

00:09:58.170 --> 00:10:01.899
However, it's also clear from the start that
she's never had a good grip on how she's able

00:10:01.899 --> 00:10:05.339
to do what she does, ascribing all of it to
the Mind Stone's influence.

00:10:05.339 --> 00:10:09.459
It may well be that the Mind Stone simply
unlocked her own innate abilities.

00:10:10.549 --> 00:10:14.380
After touching the Mind Stone, Wanda sits
down to watch an episode of The Brady Bunch.

00:10:14.380 --> 00:10:18.010
In this particular installment, Cindy Brady
is having an argument with her brother, Bobby,

00:10:18.010 --> 00:10:19.379
about her beloved doll.

00:10:19.379 --> 00:10:23.249
As for the plot of that particular Brady Bunch
episode, the doll goes missing, and Cindy

00:10:23.249 --> 00:10:24.249
blames Bobby.

00:10:24.249 --> 00:10:28.350
The young boy professes his innocence, and
their argument spills over into the rest of

00:10:28.350 --> 00:10:31.199
the family before the real culprit, their
dog, is revealed.

00:10:31.199 --> 00:10:35.110
That episode is a subtle reminder that things
aren't always as they seem, and we can often

00:10:35.110 --> 00:10:38.630
overlook the real culprits and create undeserved
blame if we're not careful.

00:10:38.630 --> 00:10:42.799
In other words, Wanda is being blamed for
certain things that she hasn't done, and Agatha

00:10:42.799 --> 00:10:44.700
is gleefully stringing her along.

00:10:44.700 --> 00:10:48.639
The episode also has a slightly more obvious
connection to WandaVision: the doll that the

00:10:48.639 --> 00:10:52.959
Vision uses to practice changing diapers on
in episode 3 is an exact duplicate of that

00:10:52.959 --> 00:10:53.959
doll.

00:10:54.959 --> 00:10:58.060
In episode 8, Wanda has a flashback to the
moment she visited S.W.O.R.D.

00:10:58.060 --> 00:10:59.060
headquarters.

00:10:59.060 --> 00:11:03.549
In this heartbreaking scene, she's led to
the Vision's body and allowed to examine it.

00:11:03.549 --> 00:11:08.739
"I can't feel you."

00:11:08.739 --> 00:11:13.019
Wanda's line is a callback to the Vision's
last words to her in Avengers: Infinity War.

00:11:13.019 --> 00:11:16.990
After agreeing to destroy Vision to keep his
Infinity Stone away from Thanos, Vision tells

00:11:16.990 --> 00:11:17.990
her.

00:11:17.990 --> 00:11:18.990
"You could never hurt me."

00:11:18.990 --> 00:11:20.850
“I just feel you."

00:11:20.850 --> 00:11:22.739
Wanda revisiting that line at S.W.O.R.D.

00:11:22.739 --> 00:11:25.490
HQ is a sad acknowledgment of the end of their
connection.

00:11:26.540 --> 00:11:28.790
In episode 8's post-credits scene, S.W.O.R.D.

00:11:28.790 --> 00:11:31.740
director Tyler Hayward reveals that his team
has had a breakthrough.

00:11:31.740 --> 00:11:35.559
They've powered up the dead Vision's body
using energy channeled from an object directly

00:11:35.559 --> 00:11:37.540
affected by Wanda's magic.

00:11:37.540 --> 00:11:41.389
As it turns out, all Hayward has wanted all
along is a way to revive and control this

00:11:41.389 --> 00:11:42.480
sentient weapon.

00:11:42.480 --> 00:11:46.839
This revived Vision is a ghostly white, a
direct reference to a period in the comics

00:11:46.839 --> 00:11:51.220
when Vision was torn apart and then put back
together by Henry Pym during the late '80s.

00:11:51.220 --> 00:11:55.699
In the comics, the ghostly, barefoot Vision
was drained of all emotions for years, which

00:11:55.699 --> 00:11:57.540
led to more than a few supervillain plots.

00:11:57.540 --> 00:12:01.869
Will the same hold true for Vision 2.0 or
will it all tie up in a happy ending like

00:12:01.869 --> 00:12:04.480
the plots on Wanda's favorite tv shows?

00:12:04.480 --> 00:12:07.799
"Silly mischief that always becomes fine."

00:12:08.989 --> 00:12:11.910
WandaVision's seventh episode — "Breaking
the Fourth Wall" — brings us to the late

00:12:11.910 --> 00:12:14.930
'00s/early 2010's in terms of sitcom references.

00:12:14.930 --> 00:12:19.470
Wanda, Vision, Agnes, and even Darcy participate
in talking heads segments in which they speak

00:12:19.470 --> 00:12:24.079
directly to the audience, mirroring the mockumentary
stylings of sitcoms like Modern Family, The

00:12:24.079 --> 00:12:25.440
Office, and Parks &amp; Recreation.

00:12:25.440 --> 00:12:29.850
While the style used in episode 7 feels most
like Modern Family, that isn't where the intro

00:12:29.850 --> 00:12:30.850
comes from.

00:12:30.850 --> 00:12:34.670
With its flashes of things you'd expect to
find in a workplace like computer screens,

00:12:34.670 --> 00:12:38.709
calendars, and the I Love Wanda coffee mug
— plus the derivative theme music — it

00:12:38.709 --> 00:12:41.399
feels a lot more like the intro to The Office.

00:12:41.399 --> 00:12:44.939
Speaking of office comedies — just before
the theme starts, Wanda tries to laugh off

00:12:44.939 --> 00:12:49.179
the bizarre things happening in her home with
"It's probably just a case of the Mondays."

00:12:49.179 --> 00:12:53.769
That's not a reference to The Office, but
rather a reference to the 1999 comedy Office

00:12:53.769 --> 00:12:54.769
Space:

00:12:54.769 --> 00:12:55.769
"Uh oh.

00:12:55.769 --> 00:12:58.629
Seems like someone's got a case of the Mondays."

00:12:58.629 --> 00:13:02.230
The opening few minutes of episode 7 also
hide one more easter egg.

00:13:02.230 --> 00:13:06.489
As Wanda-themed objects appear one-by-one,
a license plate with Wanda has the numbers

00:13:06.489 --> 00:13:08.660
12,28,22.

00:13:08.660 --> 00:13:13.489
Those numbers are a reference to Stan Lee's
birthdate of December 28, 1922.

00:13:13.489 --> 00:13:17.999
Although the late comic book icon won't be
making any more cameos in future MCU projects,

00:13:17.999 --> 00:13:22.699
the subtle shout-out makes it clear that Lee
is gone, but definitely not forgotten.

00:13:23.699 --> 00:13:25.401
"Breaking the Fourth Wall" doesn't show the
brothers in their costumes, but the color

00:13:25.401 --> 00:13:28.739
scheme of their clothing does reflect those
of their comic book costumes.

00:13:28.739 --> 00:13:32.489
The green and white of Tommy's outfit look
a lot more like what he wears as the young

00:13:32.489 --> 00:13:33.489
superhero Speed.

00:13:33.489 --> 00:13:37.867
When Wanda gets her breakfast, she's understandably
freaked out about her milk's container transforming

00:13:37.867 --> 00:13:39.375
a few times.

00:13:39.375 --> 00:13:42.750
At one point it becomes a carton with the
picture of a missing child on the back, though

00:13:42.750 --> 00:13:45.009
we can't make out the child's face.

00:13:45.009 --> 00:13:48.629
Putting the pictures of missing children on
milk cartons became customary in the mid-'80s,

00:13:48.629 --> 00:13:53.100
so it's interesting to remember that the WandaVision
episode dealing with the '80s, episode 5,

00:13:53.100 --> 00:13:57.279
"On a Very Special Episode…" also happens
to be the same episode in which Vision points

00:13:57.279 --> 00:13:59.800
out that Westview's children are missing.

00:13:59.800 --> 00:14:01.540
Wanda's cereal is interesting as well.

00:14:01.540 --> 00:14:06.189
First, the brand's mascot is a cartoon clown,
and at the end of the previous episode Wanda's

00:14:06.189 --> 00:14:08.649
expanding hex radius changes the S.W.O.R.D.

00:14:08.649 --> 00:14:09.949
agents into circus clowns.

00:14:09.949 --> 00:14:12.119
Second, there's the name "Sugar Snaps."

00:14:12.119 --> 00:14:16.309
It's impossible to see or hear the word "snap"
in an MCU production without thinking of the

00:14:16.309 --> 00:14:20.009
most infamous snap of all — the one Thanos
makes to kill half the universe.

00:14:21.029 --> 00:14:25.559
As a result of Wanda's swiftly expanding hex
radius at the end of episode 6, unlike most

00:14:25.559 --> 00:14:26.559
of the S.W.O.R.D.

00:14:26.559 --> 00:14:30.510
agents who are turned into clowns, Agent Monti
is changed into the circus strongman.

00:14:30.510 --> 00:14:34.360
It's unlikely that Monti has been made into
any kind of superhero, but at the same time,

00:14:34.360 --> 00:14:38.350
when we see a guy in a comic book TV show
in a colorful, tight outfit with a big S on

00:14:38.350 --> 00:14:40.819
his belt, there's a good chance that means
something.

00:14:40.819 --> 00:14:44.160
When you combine the S with the blue and yellow
color scheme, there are a couple of different

00:14:44.160 --> 00:14:48.000
superheroes this could be referencing, and
both of them are super strong.

00:14:48.000 --> 00:14:49.999
One possibility is the mutant hero Strong
Guy.

00:14:49.999 --> 00:14:53.989
When he first joins the new version of X-Factor,
Strong Guy, like the rest of the team, wears

00:14:53.989 --> 00:14:55.809
a blue and yellow uniform.

00:14:55.809 --> 00:14:58.639
Another possibility, and a more fitting one,
is the Sentry.

00:14:58.639 --> 00:15:02.859
Like Monti's getup, Sentry's original outfit
is yellow and blue and it includes an S on

00:15:02.859 --> 00:15:03.859
the belt.

00:15:03.859 --> 00:15:08.339
Also, while he isn't introduced until 2000's
The Sentry #1, it's revealed the hero's been

00:15:08.339 --> 00:15:12.579
around since the early days of Marvel but
the entire world has forgotten about him,

00:15:12.579 --> 00:15:15.223
including the Sentry himself — just as Monti
has forgotten who he is.

00:15:17.100 --> 00:15:21.149
Understandably, because of reports that Elizabeth
Olsen's Wanda Maximoff will be an important

00:15:21.149 --> 00:15:25.079
part of the upcoming Doctor Strange in the
Multiverse of Madness, fans have been theorizing

00:15:25.079 --> 00:15:28.310
that WandaVision would have something to do
with Marvel's vast multiverse.

00:15:28.310 --> 00:15:32.260
One of the most overt signs we receive in
the story itself is the commercial in episode

00:15:32.260 --> 00:15:33.260
7.

00:15:33.260 --> 00:15:36.649
While the same man and woman who regularly
appear in WandaVision's faux commercials weren't

00:15:36.649 --> 00:15:41.689
around in episode 6, they return in "Breaking
the Fourth Wall" for an ad that mimics antidepressant

00:15:41.689 --> 00:15:42.689
commercials.

00:15:42.689 --> 00:15:45.800
The brand name of the medication is Nexus
— a word that has two meanings in Marvel

00:15:45.800 --> 00:15:48.720
Comics, both of which relate to the multiverse.

00:15:48.720 --> 00:15:52.759
There's Marvel's Nexus of All Realities, which
in the prime universe of the comics exists

00:15:52.759 --> 00:15:56.189
in the Florida Everglades and is guarded by
the monstrous Man-Thing.

00:15:56.189 --> 00:16:00.309
As the name implies, the Nexus is a gateway
to Marvel's other realities.

00:16:00.309 --> 00:16:01.949
And then there's Wanda herself.

00:16:01.949 --> 00:16:05.619
In the comics, Wanda is the Nexus Being of
the prime Marvel reality.

00:16:05.619 --> 00:16:09.989
As a Nexus Being, Wanda reflects her reality's
defining characteristics and can alter the

00:16:09.989 --> 00:16:11.489
flow of the future.

00:16:11.489 --> 00:16:16.209
In the 1998-'89 miniseries Avengers Forever,
we learn the powerful Immortus knows Wanda

00:16:16.209 --> 00:16:20.079
is a Nexus Being and fears the possibility
of her having children even more powerful

00:16:20.079 --> 00:16:21.079
than herself.

00:16:21.079 --> 00:16:25.290
He manipulates events to pair Vision and Wanda
off as a couple, in the futile hope that Wanda's

00:16:25.290 --> 00:16:27.279
coupling with an android could never produce
offspring.

00:16:28.689 --> 00:16:33.979
In episode 4 of WandaVision, we hear echoes
from 2019's Captain Marvel as Monica is resurrected

00:16:33.979 --> 00:16:34.979
during the Blip.

00:16:34.979 --> 00:16:39.149
We hear those voices and more again in episode
7 as Monica forces her way through the hex

00:16:39.149 --> 00:16:40.149
barrier.

00:16:40.149 --> 00:16:42.970
And this time, it seems clear Monica is hearing
them as well.

00:16:42.970 --> 00:16:46.709
We hear the young Monica talking with her
late mother about helping Carol Danvers.

00:16:46.709 --> 00:16:50.190
We hear Nick Fury tell her that she can only
follow the adult heroes if she can learn to

00:16:50.190 --> 00:16:51.620
glow like her Aunt Carol.

00:16:51.620 --> 00:16:53.399
And we know in the comics she does glow.

00:16:53.399 --> 00:16:57.459
We also hear different people like Dr. Harley
and Jimmy Woo speaking about the death of

00:16:57.459 --> 00:16:58.759
Monica's mother.

00:16:58.759 --> 00:17:00.790
And of course, we hear a refrain of this line:

00:17:00.790 --> 00:17:02.079
"Your mom's lucky.

00:17:02.079 --> 00:17:06.860
When they were handing out kids, they gave
her the toughest one.

00:17:06.860 --> 00:17:08.740
Lieutenant Trouble."

00:17:08.740 --> 00:17:10.620
"You remembered!"

00:17:10.620 --> 00:17:14.220
It's these words that give Monica the strength
to push the rest of the way through the barrier.

00:17:15.220 --> 00:17:19.699
Piercing the hex barrier gives Monica superpowers,
though it isn't clear exactly what those powers

00:17:19.699 --> 00:17:20.699
mean.

00:17:20.699 --> 00:17:24.409
We see her eyes glow blue several times, and
she recovers so quickly from Wanda's assault

00:17:24.409 --> 00:17:27.630
toward the end of episode 7 that even Wanda
is shocked.

00:17:27.630 --> 00:17:31.519
In the comics, Monica is quietly one of the
most powerful heroes in Marvel.

00:17:31.519 --> 00:17:35.730
She's had a number of aliases, the most recent
being Spectrum, and after she's bombarded

00:17:35.730 --> 00:17:39.639
with energy from another dimension, she gains
the ability to transform into any form of

00:17:39.639 --> 00:17:40.700
energy at will.

00:17:40.700 --> 00:17:45.310
She's also had another alias that's been hinted
onscreen before: Photon, just like Monica's

00:17:45.310 --> 00:17:46.779
mother's call sign in the MCU.

00:17:47.820 --> 00:17:51.269
By the end of "Breaking the Fourth Wall,"
a lot of fans' suspicions that Agnes might

00:17:51.269 --> 00:17:54.240
be more than a friendly neighbor seem to have
been confirmed.

00:17:54.240 --> 00:17:57.919
Wanda's own suspicions are sparked when Agnes
leads her into her home and Wanda notices

00:17:57.919 --> 00:18:00.149
the twins are nowhere to be seen.

00:18:00.149 --> 00:18:02.860
She then sees a large, loud insect on the
window shade.

00:18:02.860 --> 00:18:07.120
She asks Agnes where the boys are, and Agnes
answers that they're "probably just playing

00:18:07.120 --> 00:18:08.330
in the basement."

00:18:08.330 --> 00:18:12.149
Wanda goes down to the basement to look for
them, and we get the episode's biggest reveal.

00:18:12.149 --> 00:18:15.549
This all unfolds like a tribute to The Silence
of the Lambs.

00:18:15.549 --> 00:18:19.149
Toward the end of that film the hero, Clarice
Starling, believes she's just doing a routine

00:18:19.149 --> 00:18:22.960
interview about the first victim of notorious
serial killer Buffalo Bill.

00:18:22.960 --> 00:18:26.240
She's speaking to a man who now lives where
the person she wants to interview used to

00:18:26.240 --> 00:18:27.240
live.

00:18:27.240 --> 00:18:30.480
She's already suspicious of his manner and
some of the things she sees in his house.

00:18:30.480 --> 00:18:34.159
But when she notices a large exotic moth,
something the killer leaves behind in his

00:18:34.159 --> 00:18:36.779
victims' throats, that seals the deal.

00:18:36.779 --> 00:18:40.070
She pulls her gun on him and he runs to the
basement, where they have their final showdown.

00:18:41.240 --> 00:18:46.139
When Wanda and Agnes confront each other in
Agnes' basement, we learn Agnes isn't Agnes.

00:18:46.139 --> 00:18:47.980
She's the witch Agatha Harkness.

00:18:47.980 --> 00:18:52.549
We then get a montage of moments throughout
the series when it's revealed Agatha was manipulating

00:18:52.549 --> 00:18:53.549
events.

00:18:53.549 --> 00:18:56.350
It isn't clear, however, exactly what Agatha
is after.

00:18:56.350 --> 00:18:57.970
Harkness isn't a villain in the comics.

00:18:57.970 --> 00:19:02.050
In fact, at different times, Harkness has
been Wanda's ally and even a mentor.

00:19:02.050 --> 00:19:06.240
While not really a superhero in the traditional
sense, she has surfaced every now and then

00:19:06.240 --> 00:19:09.150
to aid superheroes during different mystical
crises.

00:19:09.150 --> 00:19:13.260
So while it's finally confirmed that Agnes
is Agatha, we still don't know why she's there.

00:19:13.260 --> 00:19:17.070
It also isn't clear just what she means when
she says it was her "all along."

00:19:18.070 --> 00:19:21.149
Just before Agatha reveals herself in the
basement, Wanda notices what appears to be

00:19:21.149 --> 00:19:24.700
a very serious looking tome shimmering with
crimson energy.

00:19:24.700 --> 00:19:27.919
We're not sure exactly what this is, though
it could be the Darkhold.

00:19:27.919 --> 00:19:32.260
The Darkhold is probably the most infamous
magic tome in the history of Marvel Comics,

00:19:32.260 --> 00:19:36.940
sought after by the likes of Doctor Strange
and even the vampire king Count Dracula himself.

00:19:36.940 --> 00:19:41.250
The thing is, we've already seen the Darkhold
in the MCU — in both Agents of S.H.I.E.L.D.

00:19:41.250 --> 00:19:43.799
and Runaways — and it didn't look like this.

00:19:43.799 --> 00:19:47.429
But on the other hand, it isn't completely
clear exactly what canonical weight a lot

00:19:47.429 --> 00:19:50.100
of the Marvel TV series have in the MCU.

00:19:50.100 --> 00:19:54.259
Whatever it is, when we see Agatha use magic,
it's accompanied by purple energy.

00:19:54.259 --> 00:19:57.620
The energy coming out of the book, on the
other hand, is more red like Wanda's.

00:19:57.620 --> 00:20:02.730
Also, the design on the book's cover features
two sets of adjoined rings, kind of like...

00:20:02.730 --> 00:20:03.730
twins?

00:20:03.730 --> 00:20:05.740
And Wanda's boys are nowhere to be seen.

00:20:05.740 --> 00:20:09.889
Whatever the book is, whether it's the Darkhold
or something else, it could be acting as the

00:20:09.889 --> 00:20:11.600
twins' prison.

00:20:11.600 --> 00:20:13.059
Don't change that channel.

00:20:13.059 --> 00:20:16.720
WandaVision is continuing to reinvent the
wheel for the Marvel Cinematic Universe, taking

00:20:16.720 --> 00:20:18.440
the reality-warping series into a 1990s pastiche.

00:20:20.390 --> 00:20:24.169
WandaVision's sixth episode — "All-New Halloween
Spooktacular!" — takes a leap over the '90s

00:20:24.169 --> 00:20:26.539
to deposit us in the early aughts.

00:20:26.539 --> 00:20:30.820
With its faux home video camera work and pop
punk theme, the intro to episode 6 echoes

00:20:30.820 --> 00:20:35.129
the intro to the sitcom Malcolm in the Middle,
which premiered in early 2000 and concluded

00:20:35.129 --> 00:20:36.529
in 2006.

00:20:36.529 --> 00:20:40.370
The intro song includes lyrics reflecting
the bizarre events in Westview like "Don't

00:20:40.370 --> 00:20:43.600
try to fight the chaos" and, later, "What
if it's all an illusion?"

00:20:43.600 --> 00:20:46.890
Amusingly, the actual theme song to Malcolm
in the Middle, with it's "You're not the boss

00:20:46.890 --> 00:20:51.970
of me now" refrain, also feels like it would
be suitable for Wanda's reality-warping rebellion.

00:20:51.970 --> 00:20:56.260
Like Malcolm often did with its title character,
episode 6 begins with Billy narrating, though

00:20:56.260 --> 00:20:58.640
his inner dialogue doesn't last past the first
scene.

00:20:59.640 --> 00:21:02.580
Young Avengers fans are no doubt hoping that
Billy and his brother Tommy will eventually

00:21:02.580 --> 00:21:05.590
grow to become their super-powered comic book
counterparts.

00:21:05.590 --> 00:21:09.240
In the comics Billy is Wiccan, a mystical
hero and one of the founding members of the

00:21:09.240 --> 00:21:10.529
Young Avengers.

00:21:10.529 --> 00:21:14.850
Meanwhile Tommy is, like Quicksilver, a mutant
with the gift of super speed whose codename

00:21:14.850 --> 00:21:16.580
is, creatively, Speed.

00:21:16.580 --> 00:21:20.409
Whether or not Billy and Tommy appear in the
MCU after WandaVision, fans can at least say

00:21:20.409 --> 00:21:24.279
they got a chance to see them use their powers
and dress in their — for the most part — comic

00:21:24.279 --> 00:21:26.090
book-accurate costumes.

00:21:26.090 --> 00:21:29.211
Billy's costume — which he wears throughout
the episode — is Wiccan's costume in the

00:21:29.211 --> 00:21:30.460
comics.

00:21:30.460 --> 00:21:34.350
Pietro gives Tommy a smaller version of his
own costume, which is not what Speed wears

00:21:34.350 --> 00:21:37.309
in the comics -- but it's still fitting, considering
his powers.

00:21:37.309 --> 00:21:41.590
You may also notice that just before Pietro
starts wreaking havoc in the suburban neighborhood

00:21:41.590 --> 00:21:44.260
with his nephews, he and Tommy share a Top
Gun reference.

00:21:44.643 --> 00:21:48.970
Episode 6 gives us some scenes between Pietro
and Wanda after the bombshell cameo at the

00:21:48.970 --> 00:21:54.470
end of episode 5, and unsurprisingly there
are a lot of obvious callbacks to 2015's Avengers:

00:21:54.470 --> 00:21:55.470
Age of Ultron.

00:21:55.470 --> 00:22:00.129
Sokovia is mentioned a few times, including
Wanda saying her outfit is a "Sokovian fortune

00:22:00.129 --> 00:22:04.200
teller" and the quick flashback to the young
Wanda and Pietro trick-or-treating in their

00:22:04.200 --> 00:22:05.200
homeland.

00:22:05.200 --> 00:22:08.559
Wanda mentions their childhood growing up
in an orphanage together, and after she calls

00:22:08.559 --> 00:22:12.750
Pietro a bad influence, the brother fires
back, "who beefed in your borscht?" — with

00:22:12.750 --> 00:22:16.470
borscht being from the same region of Europe
where you would find Sokovia, if it were a

00:22:16.470 --> 00:22:17.799
real place.

00:22:17.799 --> 00:22:21.100
Right after that exchange, the twins both
ask each other about the disappearances of

00:22:21.100 --> 00:22:25.450
their accents — something Wanda slips back
into briefly in the previous episode.

00:22:25.450 --> 00:22:28.169
And of course there are the references to
Pietro's death.

00:22:28.169 --> 00:22:32.120
Early in the episode, Tommy teases Billy,
saying that his twin is afraid that Pietro's

00:22:32.120 --> 00:22:37.020
a vampire — a creature who, possibly like
Pietro, is neither dead nor alive.

00:22:37.020 --> 00:22:42.419
"You get killed -- walk it off."

00:22:42.419 --> 00:22:46.460
At the Town Square Scare, Pietro tells his
sister he remembers being "shot like a chump,"

00:22:46.460 --> 00:22:49.690
and later she has a brief vision of him pale
and riddled with bullet holes.

00:22:49.690 --> 00:22:55.389
"You didn't see that coming."

00:22:55.389 --> 00:22:59.340
One thing worth noting is that Pietro says
he was "shot like a chump for no reason at

00:22:59.340 --> 00:23:04.220
all," when in fact in Age of Ultron he sacrifices
himself in order to save Hawkeye and a Sokovian

00:23:04.220 --> 00:23:06.990
boy -- which seems like a pretty worthy cause
to us.

00:23:07.990 --> 00:23:11.299
"All-New Halloween Spooktacular" finally gives
us Wanda and Vision in their old school Marvel

00:23:11.299 --> 00:23:13.120
comic book get-ups.

00:23:13.120 --> 00:23:16.409
Images of the pair in their half-baked costumes
were some of the first photos released for

00:23:16.409 --> 00:23:17.559
the series.

00:23:17.559 --> 00:23:21.570
Billy appropriately thinks his mother is dressed
as "Old Red Riding Hood" while Pietro compares

00:23:21.570 --> 00:23:25.690
Vision to a traffic light, a half-shucked
corn cob, and a booger.

00:23:25.690 --> 00:23:27.730
"He's what you call 'a manchild.'

00:23:27.730 --> 00:23:31.740
After Pietro suggests he join Wanda and the
boys for Halloween, Wanda says he doesn't

00:23:31.740 --> 00:23:36.120
have a costume, prompting Pietro to use his
super speed to put himself and Tommy in matching

00:23:36.120 --> 00:23:40.260
outfits reflecting what Quicksilver used to
wear in Marvel's comics.

00:23:40.260 --> 00:23:43.840
The silver lightning bolt is straight from
the funnybooks, as is the slicked-back, Wolverine-style

00:23:43.840 --> 00:23:44.840
hair.

00:23:44.840 --> 00:23:46.030
Does it look good in real life?

00:23:46.030 --> 00:23:49.020
Well, not really -- but it's still a pretty
neat thing to finally see.

00:23:50.020 --> 00:23:53.690
Some fans are understandably hopeful that
Evan Peters' presence in WandaVision could

00:23:53.690 --> 00:23:56.690
be the beginning of the entrance of the X-Men
into the MCU.

00:23:56.690 --> 00:24:00.730
While it's far too soon to say whether this
is the case, episode 6 has a couple of X-Men

00:24:00.730 --> 00:24:02.460
references early in the story.

00:24:02.460 --> 00:24:05.740
As the couple argue about whether or not Vision
will be trick-or-treating with Wanda and the

00:24:05.740 --> 00:24:08.360
boys, Pietro steps in to take the synthezoid's
place.

00:24:08.360 --> 00:24:12.629
"Big guy has a conflict, twins need a father
figure for the night -- Don't sweat it, sis.

00:24:12.629 --> 00:24:14.799
I got the old XY chromosome."

00:24:14.799 --> 00:24:16.100
Weird line, right?

00:24:16.100 --> 00:24:20.559
For Pietro to mention his DNA and the letter
X is a pretty obvious reference to the team

00:24:20.559 --> 00:24:21.890
Professor X built.

00:24:21.890 --> 00:24:25.570
There's another X-Men reference that's just
a little bit easier to miss a few moments

00:24:25.570 --> 00:24:26.570
earlier.

00:24:26.570 --> 00:24:29.700
"The neighborhood watch is the only thing
that stands between the trees and the toilet

00:24:29.700 --> 00:24:30.700
paper."

00:24:30.700 --> 00:24:34.379
As Vision says his line, he points in opposite
directions and his forearms cross to form

00:24:34.379 --> 00:24:35.429
a big, yellow X.

00:24:36.429 --> 00:24:39.950
The ad for "All-New Halloween Spooktacular"
is perhaps the most bizarre faux commercial

00:24:39.950 --> 00:24:41.559
we've seen yet on WandaVision.

00:24:41.559 --> 00:24:45.321
The ad uses stop-motion animation and shows
us a boy stuck on an island in the middle

00:24:45.321 --> 00:24:46.321
of the ocean.

00:24:46.321 --> 00:24:50.649
A talking shark drops in with a surfboard,
gives the boy a "Yo-Magic" yogurt snack, and

00:24:50.649 --> 00:24:51.649
leaves.

00:24:51.649 --> 00:24:54.480
The boy can't seem to open the container,
and his teeth clatter as if he's freezing

00:24:54.480 --> 00:24:56.660
even though the sun seems to be baking him.

00:24:56.660 --> 00:25:00.570
Time speeds up and he keeps trying and failing
to open the Yo-Magic snack.

00:25:00.570 --> 00:25:02.639
Eventually he withers away to a skeleton.

00:25:02.639 --> 00:25:06.379
The ad ends with a shot of the Yo-Magic packaging
and the slogan "Yo-Magic!

00:25:06.379 --> 00:25:08.179
The snack for survivors!"

00:25:08.179 --> 00:25:11.730
The ad seems to be a fusion of at least a
couple of different brands of similar snacks

00:25:11.730 --> 00:25:13.539
from the '90s and early aughts.

00:25:13.539 --> 00:25:17.210
The packaging is reminiscent of Yoplait's
Go-Gurt, while the ad itself pulls heavily

00:25:17.210 --> 00:25:20.470
from YoGo Yogurt, which used stop-motion in
its ads.

00:25:20.470 --> 00:25:22.720
As for magic being essential for survival?

00:25:22.720 --> 00:25:23.960
The message seems obvious.

00:25:24.960 --> 00:25:28.409
At the Town Square Scare, a movie marquee
gives us a couple more Easter eggs, and like

00:25:28.409 --> 00:25:32.090
many of the other references, there's a bit
of a time gap between them.

00:25:32.090 --> 00:25:33.919
Westview's Coronet Theater is showing two
films.

00:25:33.919 --> 00:25:37.890
On screen 1 is The Incredibles, which came
out in 2004.

00:25:37.890 --> 00:25:41.809
It's a fitting movie reference considering
by the end of episode 6, every member of Wanda

00:25:41.809 --> 00:25:43.419
and Vision's family has superpowers.

00:25:43.419 --> 00:25:47.999
In fact, only moments after we see the marquee,
Tommy discovers his super speed.

00:25:47.999 --> 00:25:51.299
Screen 2 is showing The Parent Trap, however,
which — assuming we're talking about the

00:25:51.299 --> 00:25:56.830
remake and not the original from 1961 — came
out six years before The Incredibles in 1998.

00:25:56.830 --> 00:26:01.330
Either way, both versions of the film feature
a pair of twins separated at birth.

00:26:01.330 --> 00:26:05.190
It's an apt reference, considering how Pietro
and Wanda have been separated since the former's

00:26:05.190 --> 00:26:10.110
death and how — in the comics, at least
— Billy and Tommy were long-lost twins themselves.

00:26:10.110 --> 00:26:15.440
Each week, WandaVision reveals new and more
interesting layers to its strange TV-inspired

00:26:15.440 --> 00:26:16.440
reality.

00:26:16.440 --> 00:26:19.880
Episode 5 is certainly no exception, with
plenty of hidden pop culture and MCU references

00:26:20.880 --> 00:26:25.509
When we return to WandaVision's strange sitcom
reality in "On a Very Special Episode," Wanda,

00:26:25.509 --> 00:26:28.880
Vision, and all the residents of Westview
have moved into the prime time programming

00:26:28.880 --> 00:26:29.880
of the '80s.

00:26:29.880 --> 00:26:33.009
In particular, the intro borrows from Family
Ties.

00:26:33.009 --> 00:26:36.880
Among other things, Family Ties is known for
making the young Michael J. Fox famous even

00:26:36.880 --> 00:26:40.419
before he became a time traveler in 1985's
Back to the Future.

00:26:40.419 --> 00:26:44.090
The most blatant pull from the series is the
initially colorless portrait that begins to

00:26:44.090 --> 00:26:47.059
be colored in by a paintbrush controlled by
Wanda.

00:26:47.059 --> 00:26:50.830
Family Ties' intro for its first three seasons
included a similar portrait.

00:26:50.830 --> 00:26:54.630
It's a nifty visual allusion to the way Wanda
"colored in" the gray and lifeless Vision

00:26:54.630 --> 00:26:57.940
that viewers briefly spotted at the end of
WandaVision's fourth episode.

00:26:58.940 --> 00:27:01.840
"On a Very Special Episode" has its share
of '80s references.

00:27:01.840 --> 00:27:03.759
But they don't all stay in the '80s.

00:27:03.759 --> 00:27:07.909
When Agnes visits Wanda and Vision, she tells
them she stopped by on her way to Jazzercise.

00:27:07.909 --> 00:27:13.100
Founded in the late '60s and still around
today, Jazzercise hit its peak in the mid-'80s.

00:27:13.100 --> 00:27:16.080
We get another purely '80s reference toward
the end of the episode.

00:27:16.080 --> 00:27:18.100
In the kitchen, Wanda tells Vision,

00:27:18.100 --> 00:27:21.419
"Well, life moves pretty fast out in the suburbs."

00:27:21.419 --> 00:27:26.720
It sounds a lot like Matthew Broderick's famous
quote from 1986's Ferris Bueller's Day Off:

00:27:26.720 --> 00:27:28.919
"Life moves pretty fast.

00:27:28.919 --> 00:27:33.690
If you don't stop and look around once in
a while, you could miss it."

00:27:33.690 --> 00:27:36.909
But some of these references don't really
find homes in the '80s.

00:27:36.909 --> 00:27:40.730
Early in the episode, Agnes yells, "Fussy
babies, meet Buns of Steel!"

00:27:40.730 --> 00:27:44.929
The Buns of Steel workout videos, while technically
originating in the late '80s, didn't really

00:27:44.929 --> 00:27:47.299
become a craze until the early '90s.

00:27:47.299 --> 00:27:50.870
We get a similar moment when Norm references
a catchphrase that hit its peak in the late

00:27:50.870 --> 00:27:52.320
'80s and '90s.

00:27:52.320 --> 00:27:54.749
"What do you think Norm?

00:27:54.749 --> 00:27:57.090
Should we surf the internet?"

00:27:57.090 --> 00:27:58.090
"Cowabunga, dude!"

00:27:58.090 --> 00:28:01.559
"Cowabunga, dude" is most associated with
the Teenage Mutant Ninja Turtles, and, to

00:28:01.559 --> 00:28:06.639
a lesser extent, off-brand Simpsons merchandise.

00:28:06.639 --> 00:28:09.779
"Cowabunga."

00:28:09.779 --> 00:28:12.919
"Huh?"

00:28:12.919 --> 00:28:16.059
"Cowabunga!"

00:28:16.059 --> 00:28:19.409
These blurrings between '80s and '90s pop
culture could be intentional.

00:28:19.409 --> 00:28:23.510
They could be a sign that, just as Vision
is becoming more aware of Wanda's illusions,

00:28:23.510 --> 00:28:25.520
the integrity of her dream world is deteriorating.

00:28:26.780 --> 00:28:31.730
In the 2016 Vision comic series, Vision lives
in the suburbs with a synthezoid wife, son,

00:28:31.730 --> 00:28:32.730
and daughter.

00:28:32.730 --> 00:28:35.720
They also, eventually, have a synthezoid dog
named Sparky.

00:28:35.720 --> 00:28:39.799
Not only is WandaVision's Sparky a callback
to the comic series, but his close call with

00:28:39.799 --> 00:28:42.080
the kitchen's power outlet is as well.

00:28:42.080 --> 00:28:45.710
Like the Sparky of WandaVision, the Sparky
of the comics initially meets a sad — though

00:28:45.710 --> 00:28:47.129
much more brutal — end.

00:28:47.129 --> 00:28:50.409
However, he's eventually brought back to the
land of the living.

00:28:50.409 --> 00:28:54.700
Ironically, considering Wanda's decision not
to revive the pooch in "On a Very Special

00:28:54.700 --> 00:28:58.509
Episode," Scarlet Witch is one of the heroes
who could bring the flying pooch back to life.

00:28:59.509 --> 00:29:02.919
In "On a Very Special Episode," WandaVision's
strange advertisers are back.

00:29:02.919 --> 00:29:06.369
This time, the ad is for Lagos, a paper towel
brand.

00:29:06.369 --> 00:29:09.889
Showing Lagos wiping up an identical spill
to what the "leading brand" is trying and

00:29:09.889 --> 00:29:14.409
failing to clean, the ad mirrors Bounty commercials
of the '80s that touted their paper towels

00:29:14.409 --> 00:29:15.659
the "Quicker Picker Upper."

00:29:15.659 --> 00:29:19.240
The name Lagos is a blatant reference to Captain
America: Civil War.

00:29:19.240 --> 00:29:22.890
It's Wanda who unintentionally incites the
film's conflict when she accidentally kills

00:29:22.890 --> 00:29:26.210
Wakandan volunteers in the Nigerian city of
Lagos.

00:29:26.210 --> 00:29:29.619
Rather than calling Lagos a "Quicker Picker
Upper," the ad ends with the slogan

00:29:29.619 --> 00:29:34.260
"Lagos For when you make a mess you didn't
mean to."

00:29:34.260 --> 00:29:37.799
Fittingly, the juice that the towels clean
up resembles blood.

00:29:37.799 --> 00:29:41.720
One thing that's easy to miss about the ad
is the reference to what's going on with Vision.

00:29:41.720 --> 00:29:44.480
Just before the end of the commercial, the
husband knocks over a glass of beer.

00:29:44.480 --> 00:29:49.360
The wife hands him a few towels as the narrator
says, "Husbands can use it too, you know."

00:29:49.360 --> 00:29:53.350
This could reflect not only how Vision begins
to assert himself, but how episode 5 shows

00:29:53.350 --> 00:29:55.679
he has a degree of control over the illusions.

00:29:55.679 --> 00:30:00.230
For example, he's able to temporarily free
Norm from Wanda's telepathy.

00:30:00.230 --> 00:30:04.190
This may also be why Agnes is so flustered
early in the episode when she isn't sure whether

00:30:04.190 --> 00:30:08.159
she's supposed to hold the babies — because
she's getting conflicting signals from Vision

00:30:08.159 --> 00:30:09.159
and Wanda.

00:30:10.159 --> 00:30:13.179
And then, of course, there's the reveal at
the end of "On a Very Special Episode" that

00:30:13.179 --> 00:30:17.320
will doubtless go down as one of the biggest
TV moments of 2021.

00:30:17.320 --> 00:30:21.610
The doorbell rings as Wanda and Vision argue,
and after Wanda answers the door we're greeted

00:30:21.610 --> 00:30:25.909
by a familiar face, but familiar in a way
that we don't expect.

00:30:25.909 --> 00:30:29.309
Before we see the visitor's face, we see the
back of his head, which sports a head of hair

00:30:29.309 --> 00:30:33.510
that looks a lot like the one belonging to
Pietro Maximoff — Wanda's brother who dies

00:30:33.510 --> 00:30:35.820
heroically in Avengers: Age of Ultron.

00:30:35.820 --> 00:30:39.919
We are, indeed, greeted by Wanda's brother,
but a different Pietro than the one most viewers

00:30:39.919 --> 00:30:41.700
had to have been expecting.

00:30:41.700 --> 00:30:45.870
Instead of Aaron Taylor-Johnson, who plays
Pietro in the MCU film, Wanda finds herself

00:30:45.870 --> 00:30:51.600
facing Evan Peters — who plays Peter Maximoff
in 2014's X-Men: Days of Future Past as well

00:30:51.600 --> 00:30:54.769
as the final two films of Fox's X-Men franchise.

00:30:54.769 --> 00:30:59.879
As Darcy points out as she notices the change,
Pietro Maximoff has been "recast," alluding

00:30:59.879 --> 00:31:04.720
to the fact that American sitcoms are famous
for their sometimes abrupt recasting of characters.

00:31:04.720 --> 00:31:08.370
One of the most well-remembered examples of
this comes from a sitcom WandaVision mirrors

00:31:08.370 --> 00:31:10.630
in its second episode – Bewitched.

00:31:10.630 --> 00:31:14.659
The main character's husband, Darrin, was
recast when Dick York was replaced by Dick

00:31:14.659 --> 00:31:18.059
Sargent in the series' sixth season.

00:31:18.059 --> 00:31:22.279
Four episodes in, and WandaVision keeps revealing
new and more interesting layers to this strange

00:31:22.279 --> 00:31:23.279
new reality.

00:31:23.763 --> 00:31:27.549
"We Interrupt This Program" opens with the
first extended onscreen depiction of the Blip

00:31:27.549 --> 00:31:32.100
— the resurrection of all the people killed
by Thanos' snap in Avengers: Infinity War.

00:31:32.100 --> 00:31:35.619
Spider-Man: Far from Home lets us know how
the event comes to be known as the "Blip"

00:31:35.619 --> 00:31:39.020
and some of the consequences of it, but this
is the first time we get a good long look

00:31:39.020 --> 00:31:41.419
at the chaos immediately following the event.

00:31:41.419 --> 00:31:45.450
Specifically, we see Monica Rambeau returning
from the dead in a hospital room where her

00:31:45.450 --> 00:31:48.200
mother was a patient during the events of
Infinity War.

00:31:48.200 --> 00:31:52.350
As Rambeau's body reforms, we hear dialogue
from 2019's Captain Marvel.

00:31:52.350 --> 00:31:53.570
"Your mom's lucky.

00:31:53.570 --> 00:31:58.330
When they were handing out kids, they gave
her the toughest one.

00:31:58.330 --> 00:32:00.370
Lieutenant Trouble."

00:32:00.370 --> 00:32:02.409
"You remembered!"

00:32:02.409 --> 00:32:06.200
It's a fitting way to start the episode, considering
one of the biggest mysteries the story presents

00:32:06.200 --> 00:32:09.030
is how the synthezoid Vision has returned
from the dead.

00:32:09.030 --> 00:32:10.659
Captain Monica

00:32:10.659 --> 00:32:13.929
Considering all the hints that have been dropped
in the previous three episodes, it's no surprise

00:32:13.929 --> 00:32:16.510
to learn that Monica Rambeau works for S.W.O.R.D.

00:32:16.510 --> 00:32:20.200
Her scenes with Director Hayward pretty strongly
imply that she was the frontrunner for his

00:32:20.200 --> 00:32:22.850
job before Thanos' snap made that impossible.

00:32:22.850 --> 00:32:26.879
But while she may not have the top position,
Monica isn't without rank in S.W.O.R.D.

00:32:26.879 --> 00:32:30.580
After Monica's security badge doesn't work
at headquarters, Director Hayward shows up

00:32:30.580 --> 00:32:33.999
and introduces her to the security guard as
"Captain Monica Rambeau."

00:32:33.999 --> 00:32:35.770
The rank isn't an accident.

00:32:35.770 --> 00:32:39.450
When she shows up in Marvel Comics, Monica
is a different kind of captain.

00:32:39.450 --> 00:32:43.399
After obtaining the ability to transform into
any form of energy, Monica becomes known as

00:32:43.399 --> 00:32:46.890
Captain Marvel, years before Carol Danvers
takes up the title.

00:32:46.890 --> 00:32:47.890
Maria Rambeau

00:32:47.890 --> 00:32:51.629
In the opening scene of the episode, we learn
that Maria Rambeau is dead.

00:32:51.629 --> 00:32:55.299
From a conversation between Monica and Dr.
Highland, it's revealed that at some point

00:32:55.299 --> 00:32:59.230
between the events of Captain Marvel and those
of Infinity War, Maria was diagnosed with

00:32:59.230 --> 00:33:00.299
cancer.

00:33:00.299 --> 00:33:04.239
When Thanos made his fateful snap, Maria had
just undergone a procedure to treat her cancer

00:33:04.239 --> 00:33:06.330
and it was deemed a success.

00:33:06.330 --> 00:33:10.639
Because Thanos' snap wiped Monica from existence,
she awakes not knowing that her mother's cancer

00:33:10.639 --> 00:33:13.950
soon returned and she died three years before
the Blip.

00:33:13.950 --> 00:33:17.750
But when Monica returns to S.W.O.R.D., it's
clear her mother isn't forgotten.

00:33:17.750 --> 00:33:22.250
As Director Hayward leads Monica into the
headquarters, they pass by a number of portraits,

00:33:22.250 --> 00:33:25.379
including one of Maria Rambeau with her call
sign, "Photon."

00:33:25.379 --> 00:33:28.440
Maria wasn't just a high-ranking member of
S.W.O.R.D., either.

00:33:28.440 --> 00:33:32.110
Hayward says she "built this place from the
ground up," which makes sense.

00:33:32.110 --> 00:33:35.779
After all, S.W.O.R.D. in the comics deals
with extraterrestrial threats, and as far

00:33:35.779 --> 00:33:39.919
as we know, Maria is one of the first few
MCU Earthlings to knowingly have contact with

00:33:39.919 --> 00:33:40.919
any extraterrestrials.

00:33:40.919 --> 00:33:42.019
Agent Woo, magical man

00:33:42.019 --> 00:33:47.330
We first meet FBI agent Woo in 2019's Ant-Man
and The Wasp when Woo is in charge of babysitting

00:33:47.330 --> 00:33:51.440
Scott Lang while Lang's under house arrest
for violating the Sokovia Accords in Captain

00:33:51.440 --> 00:33:52.889
America: Civil War.

00:33:52.889 --> 00:33:56.879
In WandaVision, he's the FBI agent waiting
for Monica Rambeau to help him with his missing

00:33:56.879 --> 00:34:00.940
persons case, and their introduction to one
another includes an Easter egg from his very

00:34:00.940 --> 00:34:02.440
first MCU scene.

00:34:02.440 --> 00:34:06.360
In Ant-Man and the Wasp, Scott uses sleight
of hand to make a playing card appear out

00:34:06.360 --> 00:34:07.360
of thin air.

00:34:07.360 --> 00:34:09.560
Later, Woo has an important question for Scott:

00:34:09.560 --> 00:34:13.389
"How'd you do it, Scott?"

00:34:13.389 --> 00:34:14.919
"Do what?"

00:34:14.919 --> 00:34:17.210
"The card trick."

00:34:17.210 --> 00:34:18.210
"Seriously?"

00:34:18.210 --> 00:34:22.240
It turns out Woo's a huge fan of sleight-of-hand,
and WandaVision reveals that he's been honing

00:34:22.240 --> 00:34:24.300
his skills since babysitting Scott.

00:34:24.300 --> 00:34:28.460
As he's approaching Monica for the first time,
he uses the same trick to produce his business

00:34:28.460 --> 00:34:29.460
card.

00:34:30.460 --> 00:34:33.630
During the montage that shows us the progress
of the joint task force investigation, we

00:34:33.630 --> 00:34:37.149
get a couple shots of Agent Woo taking notes
on a dry erase board.

00:34:37.149 --> 00:34:38.750
Under the heading "What is behind this?"

00:34:38.750 --> 00:34:41.549
Woo has written "extraterrestrials (Skrulls?)".

00:34:41.549 --> 00:34:45.020
We first meet the shapeshifting Skrulls in
Captain Marvel, and what we know about them

00:34:45.020 --> 00:34:47.500
so far makes Woo's notation very interesting.

00:34:47.500 --> 00:34:51.330
While Skrulls are traditionally villains in
the comics, Captain Marvel shows that they're

00:34:51.330 --> 00:34:54.980
just trying to survive being annihilated by
the Kree in the MCU.

00:34:54.980 --> 00:34:58.960
Captain Marvel and her allies help the aliens,
and in the mid-credits scene of Spider-Man:

00:34:58.960 --> 00:35:01.750
Far from Home, they appear to be working with
Nick Fury.

00:35:01.750 --> 00:35:04.940
So why would Agent Woo or anyone else suspect
them of something nefarious?

00:35:04.940 --> 00:35:09.079
It could be that relations between Earth and
the Skrulls have changed.

00:35:09.079 --> 00:35:12.329
Another possibility is that while Captain
Marvel seemed to imply its Skrulls were some

00:35:12.329 --> 00:35:16.059
of the last the Kree hadn't wiped out, maybe
there are more of the shapeshifters out there

00:35:16.059 --> 00:35:18.410
who aren't as friendly as the ones working
with Nick Fury.

00:35:20.400 --> 00:35:24.290
The conclusion of "We Interrupt This Program"
implies that Wanda is behind all the weirdness

00:35:24.290 --> 00:35:27.550
going on in WandaVision — at least, that's
Monica's theory.

00:35:27.550 --> 00:35:31.220
When Wanda uses her powers to hurl Monica
beyond the boundaries of Westview and she

00:35:31.220 --> 00:35:35.420
lands among the joint task force, Monica has
one conclusion:

00:35:35.420 --> 00:35:38.319
"It's Wanda.

00:35:38.319 --> 00:35:42.660
It's all Wanda."

00:35:42.660 --> 00:35:46.520
The episode ends with Wanda and Vision sitting
down with their newborn twins to watch some

00:35:46.520 --> 00:35:52.450
TV as the Jimi Hendrix Experience's 1968 song
"Voodoo Child (Slight Return)" plays.

00:35:52.450 --> 00:35:56.300
The song is a perfect cap to "We Interrupt
This Program" for a couple of reasons.

00:35:56.300 --> 00:36:00.109
While the word "Voodoo" can refer to any number
of things, in American pop culture it's often

00:36:00.109 --> 00:36:04.520
associated with witchcraft, making the song
just one of a growing list of things referencing

00:36:04.520 --> 00:36:06.220
witchcraft in the series.

00:36:06.220 --> 00:36:08.349
More importantly, there are the opening lyrics.

00:36:08.349 --> 00:36:09.349
Hendrix sings

00:36:09.349 --> 00:36:12.890
"Well, I stand up next to a mountain / And
I chop it down with the edge of my hand."

00:36:12.890 --> 00:36:14.700
Then, later in the verse, we hear

00:36:14.700 --> 00:36:19.170
"Well I pick up all pieces and make an island
/ Might even raise a little sand."

00:36:19.170 --> 00:36:23.160
If Monica is right and Wanda is behind everything
going on in Westview, then the imagery of

00:36:23.160 --> 00:36:27.040
someone chopping down mountains and using
the debris to build an island is pretty fitting

00:36:27.040 --> 00:36:29.359
for the series.

00:36:29.359 --> 00:36:33.750
WandaVision is the MCU's weirdest project
to date, full of very obvious sitcom references,

00:36:33.750 --> 00:36:36.990
and more than a few subtle hints that something
in Wanda's world is very, very wrong.

00:36:36.990 --> 00:36:39.839
The third episode is no different.

00:36:39.839 --> 00:36:43.490
Prior to the release of WandaVision, the last
time we saw Scarlet Witch she was tearing

00:36:43.490 --> 00:36:47.480
Thanos apart single-handedly, posing for a
group shot, and chatting with Clint Barton

00:36:47.480 --> 00:36:48.829
after Tony Stark's funeral.

00:36:48.829 --> 00:36:52.299
That's where we left her in Endgame, but in
this show, her main challenge seems to be

00:36:52.299 --> 00:36:53.589
in the realm of new beginnings.

00:36:53.589 --> 00:36:57.720
While working on her nursery, Wanda unintentionally
makes the butterflies of the mobile above

00:36:57.720 --> 00:37:00.150
the crib turn into actual butterflies.

00:37:00.150 --> 00:37:04.319
This is a reference to a popular Marvel Comics
miniseries, and could even be the foreshadowing

00:37:04.319 --> 00:37:06.830
for a character who has yet to be revealed.

00:37:06.830 --> 00:37:07.830
"Kicking?

00:37:07.830 --> 00:37:08.830
Already?"

00:37:08.830 --> 00:37:12.220
"It's such a strange sensation, it's kind
of fluttery!"

00:37:12.220 --> 00:37:16.910
One of the comic book series fans have been
comparing most to WandaVision is the 2005

00:37:16.910 --> 00:37:21.710
miniseries House of M. In fact, Paul Bettany
has said WandaVision was pitched to him specifically

00:37:21.710 --> 00:37:24.589
as a mash-up of the comic books Vision and
House of M.

00:37:24.589 --> 00:37:29.420
In the latter series, Wanda uses her reality
warping powers to completely remake the world.

00:37:29.420 --> 00:37:32.770
With a few noteworthy exceptions here and
there, most of the people in this new reality

00:37:32.770 --> 00:37:37.000
have no idea the world's been remade, but
one of the exceptions is Layla Miller — who

00:37:37.000 --> 00:37:40.210
comes to be known as, you guessed it, Butterfly.

00:37:40.210 --> 00:37:44.460
Layla is a mutant before House of M, and somehow
Wanda's reality-warping gives her the ability

00:37:44.460 --> 00:37:47.849
to "awaken" people into the knowledge that
they're living in a world gone wrong.

00:37:47.849 --> 00:37:51.660
Nor do we think that this is the last time
Layla is referenced in episode 3.

00:37:51.660 --> 00:37:55.371
When they're talking about what to name who
they assume will be their newborn son, Wanda

00:37:55.371 --> 00:37:57.490
jokes about hoping "it's a girl."

00:37:57.490 --> 00:38:02.220
Later, when Vision practices changing a diaper,
he uses a girl doll with blond hair — just

00:38:02.220 --> 00:38:05.720
like Layla — in spite of the fact that he
and Wanda seem to be sure their child will

00:38:05.720 --> 00:38:06.720
be a boy.

00:38:06.720 --> 00:38:11.140
The episode also keeps up the trend of breaking
up the action with a surreal fake commercial.

00:38:11.140 --> 00:38:15.290
Episode 3's advertisement pulls from old commercials
for Calgon, a water softening brand with a

00:38:15.290 --> 00:38:16.890
line of bath and beauty products.

00:38:16.890 --> 00:38:21.319
In the 70s and 80s, there was a popular series
of advertisements in which overworked, overstressed

00:38:21.319 --> 00:38:25.109
women would throw their hands up and beg for
deliverance from the Calgon brand.

00:38:25.109 --> 00:38:26.790
"That does it!

00:38:26.790 --> 00:38:29.020
Calgon, take me away!"

00:38:29.020 --> 00:38:32.060
After uttering the magic phrase, they would
be transported to a bathtub where they would

00:38:32.060 --> 00:38:33.480
be overcome with comfort.

00:38:33.480 --> 00:38:37.109
In this ad, a busy mother can't even eat her
breakfast without her kids knocking her cereal

00:38:37.109 --> 00:38:38.230
away with a soccer ball.

00:38:38.230 --> 00:38:42.540
A dog reminiscent of Vision's pet Sparky from
The Vision comic — minus the green fur — pees

00:38:42.540 --> 00:38:43.579
against the wall.

00:38:43.579 --> 00:38:46.030
Then a narrator asks her, "Do you need a break?"

00:38:46.030 --> 00:38:47.390
"You read my mind."

00:38:47.390 --> 00:38:50.450
Which is, of course, something that Wanda
can literally do.

00:38:50.450 --> 00:38:53.990
The woman in the commercial is transported
to a bubble bath where a man in a Roman toga

00:38:53.990 --> 00:38:56.020
fans her like a doting servant.

00:38:56.020 --> 00:38:59.130
Among other things, the narrator says, "When
you want to get away, but you don't want to

00:38:59.130 --> 00:39:02.609
go anywhere," which could be a description
of what's happening to Wanda and Vision.

00:39:02.609 --> 00:39:06.980
He also speaks of a place where "your problems
float away," which evokes the way in which

00:39:06.980 --> 00:39:09.220
Wanda makes things float away with her mind.

00:39:09.220 --> 00:39:13.770
The mom, likewise, blows bubbles off her hands
as if she has similar powers.

00:39:13.770 --> 00:39:16.270
Finally the product is revealed: "Hydra Soak."

00:39:16.270 --> 00:39:18.130
The slogan is "Find the goddess within!"

00:39:18.130 --> 00:39:22.230
It's a clear reference to the powers Hydra
gave Wanda, and the fact that those powers

00:39:22.230 --> 00:39:23.870
are clearly expanding.

00:39:23.870 --> 00:39:27.650
It's also intriguing that the product box
reads "Made in USA," since that's not where

00:39:27.650 --> 00:39:30.990
Hydra was formed, nor is it where Wanda was
when she was given her powers.

00:39:31.612 --> 00:39:36.170
If you're familiar with the hit sitcom Bewitched
– starring Elizabeth Montgomery as a witch

00:39:36.170 --> 00:39:39.390
who both uses her powers to solve her family's
problems and struggles to keep her identity

00:39:39.390 --> 00:39:43.619
as a witch secret — it's impossible to not
think of the show while watching WandaVision.

00:39:43.619 --> 00:39:47.240
It comes to mind early in the premiere episode,
when Wanda is putting her dishes away with

00:39:47.240 --> 00:39:48.800
her telekinetic powers.

00:39:48.800 --> 00:39:52.600
But the similarities get kicked up a notch
in the opening credits of episode 2, when

00:39:52.600 --> 00:39:56.990
instead of using The Dick Van Dyke Show for
inspiration, WandaVision uses animation reminiscent

00:39:56.990 --> 00:39:58.730
of Bewitched's unique style.

00:39:58.730 --> 00:40:02.670
It's a particularly fitting show to choose
to pull from for episode 2 for a few reasons.

00:40:02.670 --> 00:40:06.430
One of the most obvious is the fact that both
shows deal with witchcraft.

00:40:06.430 --> 00:40:10.089
Another is that just as episode 2 ends with
the world of WandaVision changing from black

00:40:10.089 --> 00:40:14.930
and white to color, Bewitched began its life
as a black and white show in 1964 and changed

00:40:14.930 --> 00:40:16.210
to color in 1966.

00:40:17.380 --> 00:40:21.569
Early in episode 2 we get color mixed in with
the black and white, and it's as striking

00:40:21.569 --> 00:40:23.180
as it is meaningful.

00:40:23.180 --> 00:40:26.490
After practicing the magic act with Vision,
Wanda is straightening up the living room

00:40:26.490 --> 00:40:28.780
when she hears a whirring and a bang from
outside.

00:40:28.780 --> 00:40:31.780
In the bushes of her front yard she finds
a toy helicopter.

00:40:31.780 --> 00:40:35.230
Unlike everything else in this black and white
world, the helicopter shows some color.

00:40:35.230 --> 00:40:37.619
It's red and gold, with some metallic silver
mixed in.

00:40:37.619 --> 00:40:40.530
The number 57 is on its side, and the S.W.O.R.D.

00:40:40.530 --> 00:40:42.010
symbol is painted on the front.

00:40:42.010 --> 00:40:44.859
The number on the helicopter isn't randomly
chosen.

00:40:44.859 --> 00:40:49.720
The first comic book appearance of the Vision
comes in 1968's Avengers #57.

00:40:49.720 --> 00:40:54.160
And once again, just as the Toast Mate 2000
reminds us of Tony Stark, for some reason

00:40:54.160 --> 00:40:57.839
or another, the helicopter happens to be painted
in Iron Man's classic colors.

00:40:58.839 --> 00:41:02.839
Like the premiere WandaVision episode, the
second episode treats us to another faux advertisement.

00:41:02.839 --> 00:41:06.790
This time, instead of a toaster, we get a
watch manufactured by a company called Strücker,

00:41:06.790 --> 00:41:09.319
and the commercial is chock full of Easter
eggs.

00:41:09.319 --> 00:41:11.350
First, there's the most obvious reference.

00:41:11.350 --> 00:41:15.230
Baron Von Strucker — whose short MCU life
ends with Avengers: Age of Ultron — is in

00:41:15.230 --> 00:41:18.750
charge of the experiments which give Wanda
and her late brother Pietro their powers.

00:41:18.750 --> 00:41:23.420
He's a part of Hydra, whose sinister logo
is visible on the watch face.

00:41:23.420 --> 00:41:27.539
Aligned as they are, the watch hands can't
help but bear a resemblance to Loki's scepter,

00:41:27.539 --> 00:41:29.900
from which Wanda and Pietro's powers spring.

00:41:29.900 --> 00:41:31.049
Then there's the time.

00:41:31.049 --> 00:41:34.789
The watch is showing the time of 2:42, and
if you're looking for Easter eggs in Marvel

00:41:34.789 --> 00:41:36.930
media, pay attention to numbers.

00:41:36.930 --> 00:41:42.000
Consequentially, 1984's Avengers #242 is a
pretty important one for the team — and

00:41:42.000 --> 00:41:44.390
for Vision and Scarlet Witch in particular.

00:41:44.390 --> 00:41:48.480
The pair are reunited when Vision's body finally
is restored after injuries he'd suffered during

00:41:48.480 --> 00:41:49.480
a previous adventure.

00:41:50.480 --> 00:41:53.680
Early in episode 2, Wanda and Vision practice
their magic act for the talent show in the

00:41:53.680 --> 00:41:57.609
living room, and later we get to see that
act completely go off the rails when swallowing

00:41:57.609 --> 00:42:01.280
a piece of chewing gum messes with Vision's
insides so much that he acts like a raving

00:42:01.280 --> 00:42:02.280
drunk.

00:42:02.280 --> 00:42:03.280
"Is this your card?

00:42:03.280 --> 00:42:04.280
Is this your card?

00:42:04.280 --> 00:42:06.030
Is this your card?

00:42:06.030 --> 00:42:07.030
Is this your card?

00:42:07.030 --> 00:42:08.030
… Is this your card?

00:42:08.030 --> 00:42:10.589
In both scenes we also see what Vision calls
the Cabinet of Mysteries, equipped with a

00:42:10.589 --> 00:42:13.500
secret door to let its occupant seem to have
disappeared.

00:42:13.500 --> 00:42:17.470
Adorning the cabinet is a picture of the Mind
Stone stuck to Vision's head.

00:42:17.470 --> 00:42:20.130
It's not just an Easter egg, but a meaningful
reference.

00:42:20.130 --> 00:42:24.570
His Mind Stone was destroyed off-camera by
Thanos, and the Stone from the past was presumably

00:42:24.570 --> 00:42:26.339
returned by Captain America.

00:42:26.339 --> 00:42:30.190
The purpose of the cabinet as a device to
make things disappear and reappear might also

00:42:30.190 --> 00:42:31.840
have some significance.

00:42:31.840 --> 00:42:35.510
After all, both the Mind Stone and Vision
are things that were supposed to disappear,

00:42:35.510 --> 00:42:36.510
but didn't.

00:42:36.510 --> 00:42:37.510
At least not completely.

00:42:38.510 --> 00:42:42.490
When Wanda and Dottie confront each other
in the middle of episode 2, the 1965 hit single

00:42:42.490 --> 00:42:45.760
"Help Me, Rhonda" by the Beach Boys is playing
on a nearby radio.

00:42:45.760 --> 00:42:49.400
It may seem like a song chosen just because
of the era, until you think about both the

00:42:49.400 --> 00:42:51.839
name of the song and some of the lyrics.

00:42:51.839 --> 00:42:55.380
First of all, there's the title — "Rhonda"
sounds a lot like "Wanda."

00:42:55.380 --> 00:42:57.470
Then there's what the Beach Boys are singing.

00:42:57.470 --> 00:43:01.609
On the surface, the song is about a man devastated
by his fiancee cheating on him, and asking

00:43:01.609 --> 00:43:03.559
Rhonda to "get her out" of his heart.

00:43:03.559 --> 00:43:07.180
But if you think of it in the context of Vision
and Wanda's shared history, it becomes a much

00:43:07.180 --> 00:43:10.080
darker story, particularly with these lyrics:

00:43:10.080 --> 00:43:14.510
"She was gonna be my wife/And I was gonna
be her man/But she let another guy come between

00:43:14.510 --> 00:43:16.789
us/And it shattered our plans."

00:43:16.789 --> 00:43:20.420
The last thing Vision and Wanda talk about
before being attacked by the Black Order in

00:43:20.420 --> 00:43:24.089
Avengers: Infinity War is making their relationship
more permanent.

00:43:24.089 --> 00:43:25.750
Then "another guy," i.e.

00:43:25.750 --> 00:43:28.860
Thanos, shatters not only their plans, but
literally shatters Vision.

00:43:29.512 --> 00:43:33.674
As soon as preview images began popping up
for WandaVision, it became clear the show

00:43:33.674 --> 00:43:37.109
was taking part of its inspiration from a
fan favorite — but short-lived — comic

00:43:37.109 --> 00:43:38.109
book series.

00:43:38.109 --> 00:43:42.859
In early 2016, Marvel Comics released the
first issue of Tom King and Gabriel Hernandez

00:43:42.859 --> 00:43:44.049
Walta's Vision.

00:43:44.049 --> 00:43:48.180
The synthezoid had enjoyed a couple of miniseries
before then, but this one was unique.

00:43:48.180 --> 00:43:52.180
This Vision comic also found its title character
trying to build an idyllic and ordinary life

00:43:52.180 --> 00:43:53.700
in a quiet suburb.

00:43:53.700 --> 00:43:57.691
Rather than being married to Scarlet Witch,
however, Vision builds his family of synthezoids

00:43:57.691 --> 00:44:01.150
— his wife Virginia, his son Vin, and his
daughter Viv.

00:44:01.150 --> 00:44:04.670
Unlike the heroes of WandaVision, the synthezoid
and his family make no attempt to hide what

00:44:04.670 --> 00:44:08.500
they are in the Vision comic, which leads
to more than a few complications.

00:44:08.500 --> 00:44:12.010
No spoilers, but here's hoping WandaVision
has a happier ending.

00:44:13.010 --> 00:44:16.660
The style of the first episode of WandaVision
is based on sitcoms of the '50s and early

00:44:16.660 --> 00:44:20.640
'60s, and the faux intro includes a funny
Easter egg calling back to one of those classic

00:44:20.640 --> 00:44:23.130
programs – The Dick Van Dyke Show.

00:44:23.130 --> 00:44:26.950
The reference isn't so much about what happens,
though, as much as it is about what doesn't

00:44:26.950 --> 00:44:27.950
happen.

00:44:27.950 --> 00:44:31.130
Fans of the The Dick Van Dyke Show will likely
notice the similarity in theme music with

00:44:31.130 --> 00:44:32.130
WandaVision's.

00:44:32.130 --> 00:44:35.829
They'll also remember that in some episodes
of The Dick Van Dyke Show, the intro includes

00:44:35.829 --> 00:44:39.190
the titular star tripping over the ottoman
in his living room and taking a tumble.

00:44:39.190 --> 00:44:43.430
There was also an alternate version used occasionally
where Dick sidesteps the ottoman.

00:44:43.430 --> 00:44:47.530
In WandaVision's intro, Vision almost finds
himself making the same mistake as he's carrying

00:44:47.530 --> 00:44:49.240
Wanda into their living room.

00:44:49.240 --> 00:44:52.920
Unlike Van Dyke, however, Vision's powers
allow him to phase right through the precariously

00:44:52.920 --> 00:44:56.230
placed furniture and deliver Wanda to the
living room without injury.

00:44:56.230 --> 00:45:01.119
"Laughing at people in pain is a manifestation
of a deep-seated hostility."

00:45:01.119 --> 00:45:04.110
He slips and smacks his head on the table.

00:45:04.110 --> 00:45:05.110
Laughter.

00:45:06.110 --> 00:45:08.609
There's a lot of twin imagery in the first
two episodes of WandaVision, usually in the

00:45:08.609 --> 00:45:11.780
form of matching sets of jewelry, furniture,
and other objects.

00:45:11.780 --> 00:45:13.930
And in some cases, the matching pair is missing.

00:45:13.930 --> 00:45:18.260
For example, when Agnes introduces herself
to Wanda, she notices Wanda doesn't wear a

00:45:18.260 --> 00:45:21.730
wedding ring — though Wanda takes care of
this toward the end of the episode.

00:45:21.730 --> 00:45:25.730
There's also the two lobsters that Wanda accidentally
sends flying out of her kitchen window that

00:45:25.730 --> 00:45:28.089
subsequently Vision can't find.

00:45:28.089 --> 00:45:31.800
There's the couple's twin beds and other twin
sets of furniture, and even the matching pieces

00:45:31.800 --> 00:45:33.670
of toast in the Toast Mate 2000 commercial.

00:45:34.790 --> 00:45:38.020
Both of WandaVision's first two episodes include
a fake advertisement.

00:45:38.020 --> 00:45:41.170
In Episode 1, we get an ad for the Toast Mate
2000.

00:45:41.170 --> 00:45:44.400
Among other things, it gives us the first
indication that WandaVision could be taking

00:45:44.400 --> 00:45:48.360
place in the same world as the rest of the
MCU, as we find out the device is built by

00:45:48.360 --> 00:45:49.549
Stark Industries.

00:45:49.549 --> 00:45:53.829
"The all-new Toast Mate 2000, by Stark Industries."

00:45:53.829 --> 00:45:58.660
The Toast Mate 2000 is also a clever reference
to both Vision and Wanda's comic book twin

00:45:58.660 --> 00:45:59.660
sons.

00:45:59.660 --> 00:46:03.569
Vision is, after all, Wanda's "mate" — and
like the toaster, he's part machine.

00:46:03.569 --> 00:46:07.690
The toaster, of course, creates two matching
pieces of toasted bread, just like Wanda and

00:46:07.690 --> 00:46:09.410
Vision's "matching" twin boys.

00:46:09.410 --> 00:46:11.430
And who makes the Toast Mate 2000?

00:46:11.430 --> 00:46:12.599
Stark Industries.

00:46:12.599 --> 00:46:16.289
Just like how it's Tony Stark who is one of
Vision's creators in Avengers: Age of Ultron.

00:46:17.289 --> 00:46:20.789
There are a lot of allusions to witches and
witchcraft in WandaVision, which is fitting

00:46:20.789 --> 00:46:23.880
considering Wanda's alter ego is the Scarlet
Witch.

00:46:23.880 --> 00:46:27.520
One such Easter egg is part of the format
for the WandaVision show-within-a-show..

00:46:27.520 --> 00:46:31.420
At the end of both of the first two WandaVision
episodes, the couple is framed in a hexagon

00:46:31.420 --> 00:46:32.780
as the end credits roll.

00:46:32.780 --> 00:46:35.880
That's not something commonly seen in sitcoms
of any era.

00:46:35.880 --> 00:46:39.069
One explanation is that it's referencing Wanda's
powers.

00:46:39.069 --> 00:46:42.690
While we've never heard them described this
way in the MCU movies or TV shows, in the

00:46:42.690 --> 00:46:45.940
comics Wanda's ranged blasts are called "Hex
Spheres."

00:46:45.940 --> 00:46:49.670
Not to mention that a "hex" commonly means
a spell or curse cast by a witch.

00:46:50.628 --> 00:46:55.030
When Agnes takes it upon herself to help Wanda
create a memorable romantic evening in Episode

00:46:55.030 --> 00:46:58.400
1, she uses tips from a women's magazine called
Glamorous.

00:46:58.400 --> 00:47:02.211
Now, you could understandably assume there's
nothing much to make of that title... until

00:47:02.211 --> 00:47:03.930
we get to Episode 2.

00:47:03.930 --> 00:47:07.619
Wanda and Vision volunteer to be part of the
talent show benefiting Westview Elementary

00:47:07.619 --> 00:47:09.369
and they put on a magic act.

00:47:09.369 --> 00:47:12.020
Vision plays the magician and Wanda plays
his lovely assistant, Glamour.

00:47:12.020 --> 00:47:16.970
"I am Glamour and he's Illusion!"

00:47:16.970 --> 00:47:19.250
This is yet another reference to witchcraft.

00:47:19.250 --> 00:47:22.369
While usually when we talk about "glamour"
we're talking about someone's attractiveness

00:47:22.369 --> 00:47:24.960
or their lifestyle, there's another meaning
for the word.

00:47:24.960 --> 00:47:29.890
A glamour can also mean a mystical enchantment
— in particular, it's typically an illusion.

00:47:29.890 --> 00:47:32.310
For a perfect MCU example, think of Loki.

00:47:32.310 --> 00:47:36.350
When Loki disguises himself as someone else,
that would be called a glamour.

00:47:36.350 --> 00:47:39.730
And considering how much you come to question
the reality of what's going on in WandaVision,

00:47:39.730 --> 00:47:40.829
that word carries some weight.

00:47:41.284 --> 00:47:45.190
In the final moments of WandaVision's premiere
episode, we pull back from the screen showing

00:47:45.190 --> 00:47:47.160
the sitcom to see a desk.

00:47:47.160 --> 00:47:51.390
The screen playing the faux sitcom is surrounded
by much more contemporary technology, and

00:47:51.390 --> 00:47:54.660
someone whose face we don't see is sitting
and watching the strange show.

00:47:54.660 --> 00:47:58.349
More importantly, on the monitor to the left
of the TV screen — and on a notepad on the

00:47:58.349 --> 00:48:01.720
desk — we see a symbol familiar to Marvel
Comics fans.

00:48:01.720 --> 00:48:05.329
The symbol with the blade — as the picture
suggests — belongs to an organization called

00:48:05.329 --> 00:48:09.520
S.W.O.R.D., whose acronym in the comics stands
for "Sentient World Observation and Response

00:48:09.520 --> 00:48:10.520
Department."

00:48:10.520 --> 00:48:14.760
The agency is first revealed in Joss Whedon
and John Cassaday's Astonishing X-Men run,

00:48:14.760 --> 00:48:17.400
when they're introduced as the space counterpart
to S.H.I.E.L.D.

00:48:17.400 --> 00:48:18.400
— while S.H.I.E.L.D.

00:48:18.400 --> 00:48:19.660
deals with earthbound threats, S.W.O.R.D.

00:48:19.660 --> 00:48:20.940
handles threats from the stars.

00:48:20.940 --> 00:48:24.810
Fans have been theorizing about the MCU's
S.W.O.R.D. for years, and the symbols at the

00:48:24.810 --> 00:48:27.869
end of episode 1 let us know they're finally
here.

00:48:27.869 --> 00:48:31.290
Check out one of our newest videos right here!

00:48:31.290 --> 00:48:35.420
Plus, even more Looper videos about your favorite
MCU Easter eggs are coming soon.

00:48:35.420 --> 00:48:39.329
Subscribe to our YouTube channel and hit the
bell so you don't miss a single one.

